I've identified the problem, and I'm writing a fix.
The problem is that this is an object column, so we analyze the objects to figure out the appropriate DuckDB type, which is VARCHAR.
Then when we scan an object column, there are 2 paths, one is converting the objects one by one, this is relatively slow.
The other is a fast path for if they are all strings, where we assume the objects are all `builtin.str` types, so we can directly access the buffers.

The decision which path is taken is made too naively currently:
```c++
	case PandasType::OBJECT: {
		//! We have determined the underlying logical type of this object column
		// Get the source pointer of the numpy array
		auto src_ptr = (PyObject **)numpy_col.data();
		if (out.GetType().id() != LogicalTypeId::VARCHAR) {
			return ScanPandasObjectColumn(bind_data, src_ptr, count, offset, out);
		}
		// fast string path code ...
```
In this case, even though it produces VARCHAR, it should still take the slow path because these aren't `builtin.str` objects

